WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Generic containers and algorithms inherit behavior from the types and operations supplied to them.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.480
A comparator can define ordering incorrectly.

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.940
A hash function can affect correctness and performance.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.120
An allocator can change memory ownership and lifetime assumptions.

5
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.220
A move constructor can throw.

6
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:21.720
An equality operator can be expensive, stateful, or surprising.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.200
Type-dependent behavior is not automatically unsafe, but it must be part of the contract.

8
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.380
If an algorithm assumes a strict weak ordering, the comparator requirement should be documented and tested.

9
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:41.320
If a container needs stable moves to preserve invariants during growth, that expectation should be constrained or handled safely.

10
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.760
Iterator category is another example.

11
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.340
Code that assumes random access is not valid for every iterator.

12
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:57.160
Generic algorithms should express whether they need input, forward, bidirectional, random-access, or contiguous behavior.

13
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:02.620
This prevents accidental use with ranges that compile but do not support the required operations.

14
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.380
Exception behavior also matters.

15
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:13.560
A user-defined operation may throw during comparison, allocation, movement, copying, formatting, or cleanup.

16
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.060
Defensive generic code should preserve invariants when supplied behavior fails.

17
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.360
Tests should cover relevant failure paths where practical.

18
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.120
Representative types are essential.

19
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:34.220
Testing only with int or std::string can miss ownership, lifetime, exception, and customization behavior that appears with real application types.

20
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.920
Secure generic code is tested against the kind of variation it promises to support.

21
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.200
Type-dependent risk should be documented where it enters the API.

22
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:55.420
If the caller supplies a comparator, allocator, policy object, or callback, the documentation should describe the behavior required to keep the container or algorithm consistent.
